  "nameStory": "",
  "description": "Pine Spikes (Chroogomphus vinicolor) are small to medium-sized mushrooms with distinctive olive to brownish caps and a characteristic slimy or glutinous surface. They belong to the Gomphidiaceae family and are commonly found in New England, where they fruit in late summer and fall.",
  "idTips": [
    {
      "label": "Decurrent Ridges",
      "description": "Features blunt, forked ridges that run down the stem rather than true gills, which are olive to brown in color",
      "isPrimary": true
    },
    {
      "label": "Viscid Cap",
      "description": "The cap surface is notably slimy or glutinous when wet, which helps distinguish it from similar species",
      "isPrimary": false
    },
    {
      "label": "Stem Appearance",
      "description": "The stem is often whitish to pale above and darker below, sometimes with a subtle two-toned appearance",
      "isPrimary": false
    }
  ],
  "habitat": "Pine Spikes are mycorrhizal fungi found exclusively under coniferous trees, particularly white pines and spruce in New England's forest floors.",
  "seasonality": {
    "spring": "",
    "summer": "",
    "fall": "",
    "winter": ""
  },
  "funFact": "The name 'vinicolor' refers to the wine-colored staining that can occur on the mushroom's surfaces when bruised or aged, a characteristic feature that helps distinguish this species in the field.",
